ning the scope but:\n- if the user want to 'play' and discover the app, that's ok\n- if the user doesn't want to follow what's best, it's his/her choice\n- you may come again to your scope after working on the inventory, so from my point of view, nothing should be blocked, but we have to guide the user for him to do what's best. At the end everything should be consistent; for example, the user really have to look twice if the energy consumption is filling is really the one related to the whole inventory.","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1791383346,"nonce":"MNYDHrwDCnm/xsIuXG04k7cbbvY=","metadata":{"sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:id":"UCE_lALOULtcMc8AAAABaASN288AAAABJibCOQ","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:text":"73e4a3ed30593a829223c0fd2ef28c8952dc5cbe1a20bdb6d1843635c11f525a"},"target":"721c95612cd1fafb3df3dbb338ef45f7b6623450e1320bfb3a91d1362cd771f6","message":"I guess this is referring to:\n\"To ensure that the assessment is carried out correctly, the “Scope of the assessment“ block must be completed first. Once completed, the other blocks can be edited.\"\nThe idea is to guide the user, so option \"A. Guide, don't lock. \"is the best.\n\nFollowing LCA, you have to start with defining the scope but:\n- the user may come again to your scope after working on the inventory, so from my point of view, nothing should be blocked, but we have to guide the user for him to do what's best. At the end everything should be consistent; for example, the user really have to look twice if the energy consumption is filling is really the one related to the whole inventory\n- if the user want to 'play' and discover the app, that's ok\n- if the user doesn't want to follow what's best, it's his/her choice","files":null}]}